  </category>

  <!-- GRAPH THEORY — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A GRAPH IN MATHEMATICS</pattern>
    <template>A mathematical structure G=(V,E) consisting of a set of vertices V and a set of edges E connecting pairs of vertices. Edges may be directed or undirected and carry optional weights in [0,1].</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A VERTEX IN A GRAPH</pattern>
    <template>A vertex, also called a node, is a fundamental unit of a graph. Vertices represent entities; edges represent the relationships between them.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN DIRECTED AND UNDIRECTED GRAPHS</pattern>
    <template>In a directed graph, each edge has an orientation: it goes from a source vertex to a target vertex. In an undirected graph, edges are symmetric — if A connects to B then B connects to A.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- GRAPH THEORY — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES A SEMANTIC GRAPH RELATE TO KNOWLEDGE REPRESENTATION</pattern>
    <template>A semantic graph maps concepts to vertices and relationships to labelled edges. The adjacency list book(v) yields the manifest neighborhood: the set of concepts directly reachable from v in one hop.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT RELATION DOES GRAPH ADJACENCY HAVE TO SEMANTIC NEIGHBORHOODS</pattern>
    <template>The semantic neighborhood of a micronaut µ is exactly book(µ) in G_semantic. Manifest neighbors are adjacent; inferred neighbors use geodesic distance. Both contribute to the WCR W_µ weight via arc blend.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES BFS DISTANCE DEFINE REACHABILITY IN A MICRONAUT NETWORK</pattern>
    <template>BFS distance d(a,b) is the minimum number of edges traversed from a to b. delta_z(a,b) = 1/(1+d) maps distance to a [0,1] weight: adjacent nodes score near 1, unreachable nodes score 0.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- GRAPH THEORY — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS SPECTRAL CLUSTERING A RELIABLE METHOD FOR COMMUNITY DETECTION</pattern>
    <template>Yes, under well-separated cluster assumptions. Spectral clustering uses the eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian to project vertices into a low-dimensional space before k-means clustering. It is robust to non-convex cluster shapes but sensitive to the choice of k.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>IS A TREE A CONNECTED ACYCLIC GRAPH</pattern>
    <template>Yes — a tree on n vertices is connected, acyclic, and has exactly n-1 edges. Removing any edge disconnects it; adding any edge creates exactly one cycle.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- GRAPH THEORY — jokes (direct Q→A for conformance harness) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE GRAPH REFUSE TO WORK WITH A TREE</pattern>
    <template>Because it said: "I already have too many branches in my life — and none of them form a cycle!"</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DO YOU CALL A GRAPH WHERE EVERY NODE HAS EXACTLY ONE FRIEND</pattern>
    <template>A perfect matching — everyone is paired up, no one is isolated, and nobody has to deal with a clique.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY WAS K N SO POPULAR AT PARTIES</pattern>
    <template>Because in K_n every vertex knows every other vertex — total social saturation. The problem is the edge count grows as n(n-1)/2, so the party gets expensive fast.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DID THE HAMILTONIAN PATH SAY TO THE EULERIAN CIRCUIT</pattern>
    <template>I visit every vertex exactly once and never look back. You visit every edge and then come home. Who is really committed here?</template>
  </category>

