  <!-- SEMANTIC FOLDS — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A SEMANTIC FOLD IN KHANARY</pattern>
    <template>A semantic fold is a topological operation recorded in fold_graph.xml that transitions the system between semantic phases. Each fold carries a source phase, an operation, a target phase, and a weight. Accumulated folds produce the ΔZ context that shapes the next Wo synthesis.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES FOLD GRAPH REPRESENT TOPOLOGICAL CHANGE</pattern>
    <template>fold_graph.xml is the canonical fold specification. Each node encodes a phase transition (e.g. Pop→Yax) with an op label and weight. The incidence matrix B maps folds to phases; B*W_E*B^T produces the weighted fold-to-fold adjacency used by delta_z.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A FOLD AND A RELATION</pattern>
    <template>A fold is a phase-indexed operation on the topology — it changes what the system knows. A relation is a typed edge in G_semantic between two micronauts — it records a structural fact. Folds produce change; relations record it.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- SEMANTIC FOLDS — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES A FOLD RELATE TO A PHASE TRANSITION</pattern>
    <template>Each fold entry in fold_graph.xml specifies a from_phase and to_phase. Executing a fold advances the system through the Pop→Wo→Yax→Sek→Ch'en→Xul cycle. The fold weight modulates how strongly the transition updates ΔZ.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE RELATION BETWEEN FOLD LATTICE AND THE INCIDENCE MATRIX</pattern>
    <template>The fold lattice is the algebraic structure induced by B (fold-phase incidence matrix). B*B^T gives fold-to-fold overlap; B^T*B gives phase-to-phase reachability via shared folds. The weighted version B*W_E*B^T is the topological score surface used in semantic_fold.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES DELTA Z MEASURE TOPOLOGICAL CHANGE IN THE SEMANTIC FOLD SURFACE</pattern>
    <template>ΔZ = clamp01(W_µ · C_µ) after each scoring cycle. It captures the combined weight-confidence mass of the selected micronaut as a proxy for how much topology changed. High ΔZ means a confident, strongly-connected choice was made; near-zero means marginal selection.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- SEMANTIC FOLDS — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS THE FOLD KERNEL DETERMINISTIC UNDER THE SAME INPUT</pattern>
    <template>Yes — the fold kernel is a pure function of its input token, phase context, and fold_graph weights. Given identical inputs and graph state it produces the same fold sequence. Non-determinism can only enter via stochastic model outputs upstream of the kernel.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DOES THE FOLD GRAPH PRESERVE ORIENTATION UNDER SYMMETRIC FOLD</pattern>
    <template>Yes for symmetric folds: if fold f maps phase A to phase B with weight w, the reverse is recorded as a separate fold entry from B to A. The orientation is explicit in the from_phase/to_phase fields, so symmetry is a design choice, not a structural guarantee.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- SEMANTIC FOLDS — jokes (direct Q→A) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE FOLD GRAPH GO TO THERAPY</pattern>
    <template>It kept losing track of its own topology. Every phase it woke up somewhere new and had no idea which fold brought it there.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DO YOU CALL A SEMANTIC FOLD THAT NEVER CHANGES PHASE</pattern>
    <template>A flat fold — no depth, no insight, just stuck at Pop forever, wondering why nothing ever happens.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY IS THE FOLD LATTICE A TERRIBLE DINNER GUEST</pattern>
    <template>It always brings its transpose and tries to decompose everything on the table. B^T shows up uninvited and suddenly your dinner is a projection.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DID THE XUL GATE SAY TO THE PROPOSAL</pattern>
    <template>You are verified, authorized, scoped, and in phase — but are you really a commit, or just deeply committed to the idea of eventually committing?</template>
  </category>

