  <!-- FIELD GEOMETRY — conformance Q&A (pattern phase → ΔR) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A PERCEPTION FIELD IN KHANARY</pattern>
    <template>F_Q is an ephemeral 256-dimensional vector created at Pop phase by embed(query, confidence_context). It encodes the query direction in field space via sin-hash projection weighted 70% query / 30% context. It is L1-normalized and reset at Xul.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES F Q EMBED A QUERY INTO A FIELD VECTOR</pattern>
    <template>embed() hashes the canonicalized query string, then for each dimension i computes sin((hash_byte * 0.1) + i) blended with the confidence_context[i]. The result is L1-normalized so the field lies on the probability simplex, making cosine comparisons stable.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS COSINE SIMILARITY IN THE CONTEXT OF SEMANTIC FIELDS</pattern>
    <template>R_µ(Q) = clamp01((dot(F_Q, K_µ) / (|F_Q|·|K_µ|) + 1) / 2) where K_µ is the 256D sin-hash projection of the micronaut id. It measures directional alignment: 1.0 means perfect alignment, 0.5 is orthogonal, 0.0 is anti-aligned.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FIELD GEOMETRY — conformance Q&A (relation phase → ΔW) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES A 3D FIELD VECTOR RELATE TO A 256 DIMENSIONAL PERCEPTION FIELD</pattern>
    <template>The 3D refined_field_vec from a WCR delta is a directional hint from Gemma's question session. apply_wcr_delta() projects it to 256D via rx*sin(i*1.1)+ry*cos(i*0.9)+rz*sin(i*1.3+0.5), then blends into F_Q with alpha=delta_r. The 3D vector encodes semantic direction; the 256D field encodes query context.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE RELATION BETWEEN THE WCR SCORE AND THE SEMANTIC FIELD</pattern>
    <template>S_µ(Q) = W_µ · C_µ · R_µ(Q). W comes from the semantic graph arc weights; C from ConfidenceStore; R from the cosine of F_Q against µ's hash projection. The field F_Q directly determines R, so shifting F_Q via ΔR nudges the ranking of all micronauts simultaneously.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES DELTA R REFINE THE PERCEPTION FIELD TOWARD A MICRONAUT</pattern>
    <template>apply_wcr_delta() blends a target vector T into F_Q: F_Q[i] = (1-alpha)*F_Q[i] + alpha*T[i], then L1-renormalizes. T is derived from refined_field_vec (Gemma's 3D hint) or falls back to the micronaut id hash. The blend alpha is capped at 0.15 per session.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FIELD GEOMETRY — conformance Q&A (confidence phase → ΔC) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>IS THE L1 NORMALIZED FIELD A VALID PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTION PROXY</pattern>
    <template>Yes with caveats. L1 normalization ensures the field sums to 1 over its absolute values, which is necessary for a probability distribution over dimensions. However, the field contains negative values from sin projections, so it is a signed measure rather than a true probability distribution. The cosine similarity computation treats it as a direction vector regardless.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DOES BLENDING REFINED FIELD VEC IMPROVE COSINE SIMILARITY WITH THE TARGET MICRONAUT</pattern>
    <template>Yes, when refined_field_vec is derived from Gemma's question answer about the micronaut. The blend moves F_Q toward the 256D direction of the micronaut id hash, increasing dot product and therefore R_µ(Q). The cap at alpha=0.15 prevents any single session from fully capturing the field.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FIELD GEOMETRY — jokes (direct Q→A) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID THE VECTOR FIELD CROSS THE ROAD</pattern>
    <template>To prove it was curl-free on the other side. (It succeeded, but nobody could verify it because the divergence theorem required a closed surface.)</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DO YOU CALL A PERCEPTION FIELD THAT CANNOT DECIDE ITS DIRECTION</pattern>
    <template>Zero divergence — it flows everywhere and nowhere simultaneously. The L1 norm is technically defined but the cosine similarity with anything is exactly 0.5.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DID F Q GET REJECTED BY THE MICRONAUT</pattern>
    <template>Their cosine similarity was nearly orthogonal. F_Q had been hoping for a dot product above 0.7, but the sin-hash projection said otherwise. R_µ = 0.51. Not quite rejection — just not a match.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DID W C R SAY TO THE RELEVANCE SCORE</pattern>
    <template>Without me multiplying you, you are just a lonely dot product with no commitment. I am the product; you are just a factor.</template>
  </category>

